  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1005 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nFLOODING CONCERNS REMAIN. A FEW STRONG STORMS POSSIBLE:\n\nWe're past the peak in moisture, hence why rainfall has \ndiminished across the region to a degree. Regardless, showers \nwill continue for some areas throughout the night, and as \ndaytime heating picks up tomorrow, look for additional showers \nand a few discrete thunderstorms to develop, particularly around\nthe San Juan mountains. Excessive cloud cover today diminished \nthe threat of stronger storms, but as the forecast stands, \nclouds should break up more during the afternoon tomorrow. This\nshould provide a slightly better environment for small hail and\nstrong outflow gusts to develop. This threat does not \novershadow the ongoing hydrologic concerns. Even as we begin to \nexit this widespread stratiform-type rain pattern, creeks, \nstreams, and rivers will continue to respond to the plethora of\nrain which has fallen, keeping the concerns for river flooding \nin place through Friday. Additionally, where thunderstorms do \ndevelop, localized heavy rainfall could prompt flash flooding, \neven in less flashy areas given the saturated nature of soils.\n\nTHE WEEKEND + NEXT WEEK: \n\nDeterministic models are in agreement with a deepening closed\nlow descending into the Desert Southwest this weekend. This \nlocks in moist southwesterly flow across our CWA. This moisture \nis more your standard sub-tropical flow and less like what we \nare currently enduring. This leaves PWAT's more around 200% of \nnormal, which, although less than we've seen recently, is still \nplenty to promote a continuation of active weather. Given this\nsystem's arrival coming so quickly, the saturated atmosphere\nwill likely lean towards denser morning cloud cover. This would\ndiminish the potential for localized heavy rainfall, but most \nof the Four Corners and surrounding areas have already seen such\na significant amount of precipitation this month that it may not\ntake much, even from stratiform rain, to spark additional\nflooding and flash flooding problems. We'll forego speculating\non additional details for the time being as some of our forecast\nmay be dictated by how Friday plays out. Stay up to date over\nthe next 24 hours for additional information on this weekend's\nactive weather.\n\nAs we move towards next week, models are also in fairly good \nagreement that dry zonal flow will begin to propagate the region\nby Monday. Because of this, we will be seeing cooler than \nnormal temperatures, with the potential for overnight lows to \ndrop below freezing for a couple hours at a time. Keep in mind, \nthis is still relatively far out, so confidence remains low on \nthis matter.",
